      "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\n`Covenant` keeps its own ledger of how many BASE tokens the market holds in `marketState.baseSupply`. When BASE is sent out (via `redeem` or `swap` with `assetOut == BASE`), the code first decrements `baseSupply` by the amount and then transfers the tokens to `to`. There’s no special-case for self-transfers (`to == address(this)`).\n\nIf a user sets `to = Covenant` itself, the ERC-20 transfer is effectively a no-op (tokens go from Covenant to Covenant), but `baseSupply` still gets reduced. That creates a mismatch:\n\n```\nERC20(base).balanceOf(Covenant)   – unchanged\nCovenant.marketState.baseSupply   – decreased by `amount`\n```\n\nValidation in `ValidationLogic.checkSwapOutputs` caps BASE outflows by comparing `(requested + fees)` against `baseSupply` (not the actual token balance). After you “deflate” `baseSupply` with a self-transfer, exact-out BASE requests that should succeed will now revert with `Errors.E_InsufficientAmount`. In practice, anyone holding zTokens can do a `DEBT -> BASE` swap to `to = Covenant` and brick subsequent withdrawals (and any other flows that rely on `baseSupply`) until the mismatch is repaired by other activity.\n\nto execute the attack, \n* No special roles or governance needed.\n* Works with standard tokens.\n* Can be repeated to drive `baseSupply` arbitrarily low (up to the attacker’s zToken budget), turning a nuisance into a sustained DoS.\n\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\n**Block self-transfers on BASE-out paths** (simple, low risk):\n\n```solidity\n// In Covenant.redeem (BASE out):\nrequire(redeemParams.to != address(this), \"no self BASE transfer\");\n\n// In Covenant.swap, assetOut == BASE branch:\nrequire(swapParams.to != address(this), \"no self BASE transfer\");\n```",
